From 21b6e2b06f75e901543af7d9bda47a0be4814aa6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Galih Tama <galpt@v.recipes>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 11:39:36 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 05/39] v4.6-gpu: GPU virtual time scheduling with EMA
 modulation and cross-scheduler coupling

Port Infinity's GPU scheduling onto the 7.2 CFS-like DRM scheduler.
The upstream 7.2 scheduler already orders entities by virtual runtime
in a unified rbtree, so Infinity's interactivity signals modulate the
vruntime accumulation instead of replacing the selection machinery:

- EMA climb on the accounted GPU time in the vruntime fold, clamped to
  the budget; the fold and the completion accounting share the stats
  object lock, so the two-lock-domain race of the 7.1 layout cannot
  occur (no pending_ns drain needed).
- EMA idle decay by half-lives and submission-interval tracking in the
  fold; the 7.2 vruntime save/restore normalization provides the
  returning-entity catch-up that 7.1 implemented as an explicit idle
  compensation blend.
- EMA burst penalty: busy entities accrue vruntime faster and drift
  back; compositor-like entities with sub-8ms submission intervals get
  a quarter of the penalty (job-type awareness).
- CPU-to-GPU coupling: futex_waiting or a zero CPU EMA on the owning
  task halves the fold's vruntime growth, so interactive tasks keep
  GPU priority.
- GPU-to-CPU passover feedback: ready entities passed over in
  drm_sched_select_entity() increment their tasks' gpu_passovers,
  accelerating the CPU-side EMA decay in infinity_wakeup().

Per-CPU observability counters and the infinity_stats GPU section
track completions, accounting, idle decays, coupling activations and
passover boosts.  Completion accounting always finds the entity stats
object (jobs hold a kref), so the 7.1 entity-not-found and lock-drain
bookkeeping is not needed on this layout.

Signed-off-by: Galih Tama <galpt@v.recipes>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/scheduler/sched_entity.c |  19 ++
