From 6614de654d99db50c5c664053f60ec6d60d521f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Galih Tama <galpt@v.recipes>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 15:04:32 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 17/39] v4.8-gpu: continuous cgroup shield with cross-CPU
 detection

Replace the per-cfs_rq 50% cliff in update_cfs_group() with a quantized
share ramp driven by a task-group-wide EMA high-water mark, and add
observability counters.  Detection and enforcement are decoupled: the
engage decision reads the tg-wide max (any CPU hammering engages the
shield), while the ramp amount comes from the local cfs_rq EMA, so a
quiet CPU never reweights and the v4.7 no-oscillation property holds.
The max is published upward only on climbs, so a shared cacheline is
written only on new high-water marks.  Any decay marks the cache stale;
the next update_cfs_group() invocation (the only allowed scan site,
always under rq->lock) recomputes over tg->cfs_rq[] for all possible
CPUs at most once per INFINITY_SHIELD_RESCAN_MS (8ms = half the 16ms
empty-rq half-life).  No cpus_read_lock() is taken under rq->lock --
hotplug holds cpus_write_lock and then takes rq locks, so a reader
blocked behind the writer while holding rq->lock would AB-BA deadlock.
Instead, tg->cfs_rq[] is allocated for all possible CPUs and the scan
is safe because no RCU grace period can elapse while preemption is
disabled; offline CPUs are skipped via cpu_online() (a racy-but-benign
bit test -- a stale read self-corrects within one rescan window), since
group_ema on a dead CPU is frozen (no ticks, no decay), and a
permanently high frozen max would otherwise lock the shield ON.  The
ramp quantizes the reduction into 5pp steps, so reweight_entity() fires
at most once per bucket crossing, never per tick.  The tg cache lives
in its own aligned sub-struct (infinity_shield), strictly separated
from the hot load_avg cacheline.  A stale max is invalidated by the
decay-path stale mark and recomputed at the next scan; a stuck max from
an offline holder is bounded by the 8ms window and the worst over-
engagement is one 5% bucket on 40-50% local-EMA CPUs.  Stats expose
"Shield engages" (per-CPU counter) and "Group shields" (live count of
groups whose cached max is at/above the engage threshold) via
rcu_read_lock() + walk_tg_tree_from() at read time.

7.2-series placement: this tree stores task_group cfs_rq state in a
percpu struct cfs_tg_state, so the scan's tg->cfs_rq[i] array access
from the 7.1 series is adapted to the tg_cfs_rq(tg, i) accessor
(kernel/sched/sched.h) at the update_cfs_group() scan site in
kernel/sched/fair.c.  The "allocated for all possible CPUs" property,
the no-hotplug-lock safety argument, and the walk_tg_tree_from()
stats visitor are unchanged.  The climb publish is guarded under
CONFIG_FAIR_GROUP_SCHED because gcfs_rq->tg only exists in that
configuration.

Signed-off-by: Galih Tama <galpt@v.recipes>
